l Quoz (opened 2016): a white, Bauhaus-minded building of two levels designed by Mario Jossa of Marcel Breuer & Associates. Refined 6 Oct 2026 from press descriptions and the OpenStreetMap outlines in the ground file, not photographs. The ground shows a row of warehouses about 90 by 30 m, turned some 40 degrees, with the museum's point at its south-east end on the lane side; the old model was a free-standing white box centred on the point, half over the lane, so the foundation was set as the row's south-east unit (about 32 by 30 m) and the rest of the row drawn as the avenue's metal sheds under shallow pitched roofs, with roller shutters on the lane, the lane itself and a service lane behind, two more sheds beyond it, and palms along the lane. The foundation: white walls and coping, a recessed glazed entrance under the upper level on the lane, a ribbon window to the upper gallery and another on the south-east end, a skylight box over the double-height gallery, a paved forecourt. The ground file marks water beside the lane north-east of the row; it is probably mis-tagged and is left as plain paving. Still guessed: which unit of the row is the foundation, its footprint, heights, openings and roof light."}